       <td>TMDK</td>\n                <td>₦1,580.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>WOSBAB</td>\n                <td>₦1,580.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>ZAMSON</td>\n                <td>₦1,573.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>NIPCO WARRI</td>\n                <td>₦1,580.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>MATRIX WARRI</td>\n                <td>₦1,587.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>BULK STRATEGIC</td>\n                <td>₦1,600.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>NIPCO LAGOS</td>\n                <td>₦1,580.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>IBACHEM</td>\n                <td>₦1,555.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n\n        </table>\n\n        <br>\n        \n\n            \n        <h2 style=\"color:#d42e12;\">\n            Market News\n        </h2>\n        \n            <div\n                style=\"\n                    border:1px solid #fad5a1;\n                    padding:15px;\n                    margin-bottom:15px;\n                    border-radius:8px;\n                    \n                \"\n            >\n\n                <h3>\n                    UK Buys £674.5m Nigerian Refined Fuel, Overtaking Crude Oil\n                </h3>\n\n                <p>\n                    Refined petroleum products have emerged as the United Kingdom’s largest goods import from Nigeria, accounting for 47.5 per cent of total Nigerian goods imported by Britain. The shift marks a notable change in the composition of Nigeria's exports to the UK, where crude oil has traditionally held the largest share. The change was driven partly by a sharp reduction in British purchases of Nigerian crude.\n                </p>\n\n                <a\n                    href=\"https://petroleumprice.ng/news/articles/uk-buys-6745m-nigerian-refined-fuel-overtaking-crude-oil\"\n                    target=\"_blank\"\n                    style=\"\n                        color:#d42e12;\n                        font-weight:bold;\n                        text-decoration:none;\n                    \"\n                >\n                    Full Article →\n                </a>\n\n            </div>\n            \n            <div\n                style=\"\n                    border:1px solid #fad5a1;\n                    padding:15px;\n                    margin-bottom:15px;\n                    border-radius:8px;\n                    \n                \"\n            >\n\n                <h3>\n                    NNPCL IPO Returns: Nigeria Revives Decade-Old Plan to List Oil Giant\n                </h3>\n\n                <p>\n                    Nigeria has revived its long-delayed plan to list Nigerian National Petroleum Company Limited (NNPCL) on the Nigerian Exchange Group (NGX) President Bola Tinubu renewed the commitment during a meeting with the leadership of NGX Group on August 6. The potential listing of NNPCL could provide a major boost to market depth, liquidity and the number of large-scale investable assets.\n                </p>\n\n                <a\n                    href=\"https://petroleumprice.ng/news/articles/nnpcl-ipo-returns-nigeria-revives-decade-old-plan-to-list-oil-giant\"\n                    target=\"_blank\"\n                    style=\"\n                        color:#d42e12;\n                        font-weight:bold;\n                        text-decoration:none;\n                    \"\n                >\n                    Full Article →\n                </a>\n\n            </div>\n            \n            <div\n                style=\"\n                    border:1px solid #fad5a1;\n                    padding:15px;\n                    margin-bottom:15px;\n                    border-radius:8px;\n                    \n                \"\n            >\n\n                <h3>\n                    Oil Prices Rise After Iran Gives U.S. Conditions for Reopening Strait of Hormuz\n                </h3>\n\n                <p>\n                    Brent crude was trading at $84.37 a barrel, up 0.98%, while West Texas Intermediate (WTI) stood at $78.76, gaining 0.74%. Iran’s declaration that the Strait of Hormuz would remain closed until the United States meets a series of demands that go well beyond shipping arrangements.\n                </p>\n\n                <a\n                    href=\"https://petroleumprice.ng/news/articles/oil-prices-rise-after-iran-gives-us-conditions-for-reopening-strait-of-hormuz\"\n                    target=\"_blank\"\n                    style=\"\n                        color:#d42e12;\n                        font-weight:bold;\n                        text-decoration:none;\n                    \"\n                >\n                    Full Article →\n                </a>\n\n            </div>\n            \n            <div\n                style=\"\n                    border:1px solid #fad5a1;\n                    padding:15px;\n                    margin-bottom:15px;\n                    border-radius:8px;\n                    \n                \"\n            >\n\n                <h3>\n                    Strait of Hormuz Crisis: Gain for Federal Government, Pain for Consumers\n                </h3>\n\n                <p>\n                    The prolonged disruption of the Strait of Hormuz has created a difficult contradiction for Nigeria. Higher international oil prices can strengthen government revenue at a time when crude production is recovering. But the same global supply shock can expose consumers and businesses to higher energy and transportation costs.\n                </p>\n\n                <a\n                    href=\"https://petroleumprice.ng/news/articles/strait-of-hormuz-crisis-gain-for-federal-government-pain-for-consumers\"\n                    target=\"_blank\"\n                    style=\"\n                        color:#d42e12;\n                        font-weight:bold;\n                        text-decoration:none;\n                    \"\n                >\n                    Full Article →\n                </a>\n\n            </div>\n            \n            <div\n                style=\"\n                    border:1px solid #fad5a1;\n                    padding:15px;\n                    margin-bottom:15px;\n                    border-radius:8px;\n                    \n                \"\n            >\n\n                <h3>\n                    MEST OS Is the Digital Operating System Every Fuel Retail Station Needs —Obinna Joshua Igwe\n                </h3>\n\n                <p>\n                    MEST OS is an all-in-one retail operations platform built for the downstream oil and gas sector. It centralises the management of single and multi-station fuel retail networks. The platform centralises reporting into one system, reducing reliance on multiple communication channels.\n                </p>\n\n                <a\n                    href=\"https://petroleumprice.ng/news/articles/mest-os-is-the-digital-operating-system-every-fuel-retail-station-needs-obinna-joshua-igwe\"\n                    target=\"_blank\"\n                    style=\"\n                        color:#d42e12;\n                        font-weight:bold;\n                        text-decoration:none;\n                    \"\n                >\n                    Full Article →\n                </a>\n\n            </div>\n            \n\n        </body>\n\n        </html>\n        "
